'''''Echium webbii''''' is a species of flowering plants of the family [[Boraginaceae]]. It is endemic to the [[Canary Islands]], where it is restricted to the island of [[La Palma]].<ref name=canar>Manuel Arechavaleta, S. Rodríguez, Nieves Zurita, A. García (Hrsg.): ''[http://www.gobiernodecanarias.org/medioambiente/piac/descargas/Biodiversidad/Listas-Especies/Lista_Especies_Silvestres.pdf Lista de especies silvestres de Canarias. Hongos, plantas y animales terrestres]'' (''List of Forest Species of the Canary Islands''). 2009. Gobierno de Canarias, p. 151 {{ISBN|978-84-89729-21-6}}</ref> The species was first described by [[Auguste-Henri de Coincy]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tropicos.org/Name/100328857|title=''Echium webbii''|publisher=Tropicos.org|place=Missouri Botanical Garden|accessdate=19 September 2012}}</ref> The specific name ''webbii'' refers to botanist [[Philip Barker Webb]].

The flowers are normally blue and produced in numerous spikes in early summer, but a hybrid is sometimes seen with white or pink [[flower]]s. It grows to average height {{convert|100|cm}}, maximum height {{convert|150|cm}}.<ref name="rareplants">{{cite web|url=http://www.rareplants.de/shop/product.asp?P_ID=6545|title=Echium webbii|publisher=Rareplants|accessdate=28 January 2019}}</ref>

The plant is not very tolerant of cold, and in the wild is a shrub. It can be grown in gardens in mild areas, but requires good drainage.

